---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Curly Hair Care Guide: Moisture & Definition"
  description="Practical tips for caring for curly hair, including moisture, definition and routine basics."
  canonicalPath="/hair-care/curly-hair/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Care" title="Curly Hair Care" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-1.jpg" imageAlt="Wavy, textured hair close-up" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-care/" class="hover:underline">Hair Care</a> / Curly Hair</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Curly hair has different needs than straight hair at almost every step of a
      routine — from how it's washed to how it's dried to how often it's brushed.
      A lot of common hair advice is written with straight hair in mind, which is
      part of why curly hair can feel harder to figure out. This guide covers the
      basics that tend to make the biggest difference.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Moisture First</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Curly hair tends to be naturally drier than straight hair because scalp oils
      travel less easily down the curl pattern — every bend in the hair shaft
      slows that natural conditioning process. Rich conditioners, leave-ins and
      periodic deep treatments help offset that, and many curly-hair routines lean
      more heavily on conditioner than shampoo for this reason.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Reducing Frizz</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-6">
      <li>Apply product to soaking-wet hair for better distribution — curls absorb and hold product more evenly when hair is fully saturated.</li>
      <li>Use a microfiber towel or cotton t-shirt instead of a regular towel, since standard towel fibers rough up the cuticle and increase frizz.</li>
      <li>Avoid brushing dry curls — detangle with fingers or a wide-tooth comb when wet and conditioned instead.</li>
      <li>Consider a diffuser attachment if you blow-dry, which distributes airflow more gently across curls than a direct dryer nozzle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Building a Simple Routine</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A workable starting routine is: cleanse with a gentle, sulfate-free shampoo
      (not necessarily every wash day — many curly routines "co-wash" with
      conditioner more often than they shampoo), follow with a generous
      conditioner, apply a leave-in and styling product to soaking-wet hair, and
      let curls air-dry or diffuse rather than towel-drying roughly. Adjust from
      there based on how your specific curl pattern and porosity respond.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Sleeping and Protecting Curls</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A silk or satin pillowcase (or a loose silk bonnet) causes far less friction
      overnight than cotton, which helps curls hold their shape and reduces
      breakage over time. Loosely gathering curls into a "pineapple" — a high,
      loose ponytail on top of the head — before bed can also help preserve the
      curl pattern from one day to the next without a full restyle each morning.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Getting a Curl-Friendly Haircut</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      When you do go in for a cut, ask specifically whether your stylist cuts curly
      hair dry. Curls shrink significantly once they're fully dry and settled, so a
      cut done wet can end up shorter or less balanced than intended. A stylist
      experienced with curls will typically cut (or at least final-shape) the hair
      dry to see exactly how it falls.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Product Buildup and Clarifying</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Curl-friendly styling products — gels, creams, and heavier leave-ins — can
      build up on the hair over time, especially if you're not shampooing very
      often. That buildup often shows up as dullness, flatness, or curls that
      suddenly feel harder to define even though your routine hasn't changed. A
      monthly clarifying shampoo, used sparingly since it can also strip needed
      moisture, helps reset the hair before returning to a normal routine.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Different Curl Patterns, Different Needs</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Loose waves and tight coils don't behave identically, even though general
      curly-hair advice often treats them as one category. Looser patterns tend
      to need lighter products and can often tolerate more frequent washing
      without drying out, while tighter coil patterns usually need richer
      products and benefit from stretching techniques — like braiding or
      twisting sections while damp — to manage shrinkage and encourage more
      visible length. If general curly-hair advice isn't quite working, looking
      for guidance specific to your curl pattern is often the next useful step.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      See also: <a href="/hair-services/haircuts/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Haircuts for Curly Hair</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Patience Is Part of the Process</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      Curly hair often takes longer to feel "figured out" than straight hair,
      simply because there are more variables at play. Give any new routine a
      few weeks before judging it, and adjust one variable at a time.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
